<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iltutmish</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">seized throne <a href="/years/1211">1211</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-dynasty-delhi">Mamluk dynasty (Delhi)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iltutmish was a Turkic slave commander who seized the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1211. He consolidated the sultanate and in 1229 was recognised by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a>. He is regarded as the real consolidator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iltutmish.jpg" alt="Iltutmish" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islamic_Sultanates._Delhi._Shams_al-D%C4%ABn_Iltutmish._AH_607-633_AD_1210-1235._AR_Tanka_(25mm,_11.07_g,_6h)._Sind_type.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Iltutmish was born into a well-off family; his father Ilam Khan led the Ilbari Turkic tribe. According to Minhaj's Tabaqat-i Nasiri, his jealous brothers sold him to a slave dealer at a horse show, a story that seems modelled on the Quranic account of Yusuf. He spent his early life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> under several masters. In about 1197 the Ghurid slave-commander Qutb al-Din Aibak bought him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> for 100,000 jitals, making him the slave of a slave.</p><p>He became head of Aibak's bodyguard and was later given the important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a> of Badaun. His campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khokhars">Khokhar</a> rebels in 1205–1206 won the notice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a>, who freed him before Aibak himself was freed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Aibak died in 1210, the nobles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> appointed Aram Shah as his successor. Nobles elsewhere, led by the military justiciar Ali-yi Ismail, proposed Iltutmish instead, since Aibak had called him a son. He marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, defeated Aram Shah's forces and set up his capital there. Some nobles rebelled, and he had many of them beheaded. He then brought Badaun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Banaras</a> and Siwalik back under Delhi's control and faced rival former Ghurid slaves, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-al-din-yildiz">Taj al-Din Yildiz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-ad-din-qabacha">Nasir ad-Din Qabacha</a>.</p><p>In 1228–1229 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-ad-din-qabacha">Qabacha</a>'s territory, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmians">Khwarazmians</a> had weakened, and annexed large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. On 18 February 1229 an embassy from the Abbasid caliph al-Mustansir reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> with a deed of investiture and the titles Yamin Khalifat Allah and Nasir Amir al-Mu'minin. In 1230 he defeated Malik Balkha Khalji in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and in 1231 he besieged Gwalior, which fell on 12 December 1232. He died in Delhi on 30 April 1236.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Iltutmish freed the sultanate from a subordinate position to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>. He introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanka">tanka</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> jital, the basic coins of the later sultanate, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a> system, which assigned the revenues of a region to officers in return for military service. He is probably the first ruler of the sultanate to organise a centrally recruited and paid army.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> became a centre of Islamic power and culture under him; he patronised the historian Minhaj-i-Siraj and the Sufi Qutbuddin Bakhtiar Kaki. He completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-minar">Qutb Minar</a>, built the Hauz-i-Shamsi reservoir and in 1231 raised the Sultan Ghari tomb for his eldest son, the first Islamic mausoleum in Delhi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iltutmish” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Consolidation went faster under <b>Iltutmish</b>, who seized the throne in 1211, defeated Yildiz at the third Battle of Tarain in 1216, introduced the silver tanka as the standard coin, and organised the iqta system of revenue assignments.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Aybak, <b>Iltutmish</b> and other Turkic commanders of Ghurid origin ruled northern India for most of the thirteenth century, and the Muslim dynasties that dominated much of the subcontinent for the next five centuries trace their beginnings to the Ghurid conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the thirteenth century Jaitrasimha resisted the Delhi Sultanate, although <b>Iltutmish</b> sacked Nagda in the 1220s, and Chittor became the main seat of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Udayasimha extended the kingdom and repelled <b>Iltutmish</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-jalor">Chahamanas of Jalor</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty depended on Ranthambore, one of the strongest hill forts of north India, and alternated between submission to and revolt against the Delhi Sultanate; <b>Iltutmish</b> captured the fort in 1226, but the Chauhans regained it.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-ranastambhapura">Chahamanas of Ranastambhapura</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 12th century the Kachchhapaghatas lost Gwalior to a Pratihara family, and the region was later taken by <b>Iltutmish</b> of the Delhi Sultanate in 1232.</blockquote><a href="/state/kachchhapaghata-dynasty">Kachchhapaghata dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Mehrauli, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.52,77.18&amp;year=1211" aria-label="Mehrauli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6568 1120 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Iltutmish"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7018 1390h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7018 1390h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=28.52,77.18&amp;year=1211">What was here in 1211? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
